nning  here so this is not test of Mathematica  now this is my web browser connected to  the Wolfram cloud and it just happened  to have a notebook here that since loud  in slideshow mode that's why it looks a  bit different so there's you know just  to emphasize this there's it doesn't  rely on any local installation of  Mathematica or any plugin it's just the  browser rendering stuff on the client  side and then for evaluations when it  needs the kernel it makes requests to  the server we're in our Wolfram cloud  there there from engines running to to  make the such computations so that's  that's the final goal to make these  notebook interfaces work there most of  the stuff that I showed you here isn't  really new I think that was already  working about a year ago but we've been  really focusing on over the last year is  sort of improvements under the hood and  optimizations mostly so we've made  various performance optimizations in our  JavaScript codes that renders notebooks  so one metric I had was that I think if  you have a large notebook the initial  rendering got about twice as fast over  the over the last year we've made  improvements particularly to graphics so  that especially updates of graphics or  faster  let's see when you have a manipulate and  you drag a slider and it changes some  parts of a plot well most of the other  things stay the same that's much faster  now thanks to our use of react  JavaScript library that takes care of  updating the browser content and apart  from performance we also focused on the  download size of the JavaScript and also  CSS bundles that are needed to render  notebooks in the browser so we've made  some progress on that already but it's  it's still you know still an ongoing  process of optimizing that even further  not so much reducing the overall size of  our code because that will probably  always grow if as we add more features  but we try to be more clever about what  we really know to really need to load  initially to you know give you to render  the notebook initially and then as you  need more of the code we load that  lazily using webpack and then code  splitting and that already leads into  one really useful improvement that we  made over the last year I think is that  we we can now really render notebooks on  the server side quite well and the idea  is that we produce some static piece of  HTML that doesn't really rely on  JavaScript anymore and we can send that  to the browser right away when you  request a notebook page so that you see  something immediately or almost  immediately while we download all the  necessary JavaScript code in the  background and that eventually makes the  notebook interactive but at least to see  something we get there much quicker so  it feels more like a regular web page in  the end in terms of in terms of speed  the way this works is that whenever you  edit a notebook and then also close it  in the cloud we generates this this  static piece of HTML and also when you  upload  notebook you know either through the  upload button in the cloud or through  functions like cloud deploy from desktop  Mathematica whenever something like that  happens we generate a static HTML cache  for the notebook and then use it anytime  the notebook is visited it doesn't  happen synchronously and it takes a  little bit so if you deploy a notebook  and then visit it right away the cache  might not be there yet but if you wait a  few seconds it you know any subsequent  visit should should perform much better  the way we're doing this now has has  changed over time we've been relying on  browser rendering in the very beginning  where we would send the HTML cache from  the browser that was actually editing a  notebook to the server then we switch to  selenium running on the server but that  has it had its problem as well so now  we're using a node EGS process running  in a headless way on the server to to  render notebooks using the same  JavaScript code that you also use in the  browser yeah and that that works quite  well no I think it's not only good for  the performance of notebooks but also  for search engines because now that  there's some static piece of HTML right  on the page they can make much better  sense of the content of of notebooks and  so yeah it's good for search engines and  lastly but really trying to do here is  minimize any differences between the  static HTML that we render on the server  and what you might get eventually in in  the client-side when you render things  in a life way in the browser and one  important thing to do to make that  happen was to use the exactly same fonts  on the server and on the client because  you know that that static piece of HTML  uses you know relies on text measure  to know where things should go in a in a  completed form formula or so so it's  really important to have the same fonts  and if it you know used fonts that you  only had on your machine but we don't  have them on a server there would be  differences and what we're doing now is  basically only using web fonts in cloud  notebooks so that might be a little  surprising if you if you take a closer  look you might notice that where even if  you say a cell uses the font Arial or  something it's not actually using Arial  but just an open font that looks very  similar so you might notice little  differences but I think it it still  works well and the motivation is at  least part of the motivation is that we  have this very good consistency between  client and then server-side rendering  yep so that's that I only wanted to show  some new features that we developed  recently this one is not released on the  public cloud yet so I'm going to switch  to our test server where this is already  running this should be released in what  we call version 144 so probably in the  next week or soon after that so this is  a feature called multi click selection  it seems maybe on the surface like like  a small feature but if you're if you're  working with somewhat larger expressions  in your notebooks it's it's really very  useful and once you get used to it it  it's it's it's hard not having it  anymore so we really wanted to implement  this the idea is that you know you can  of course double click a symbol and it  will select the whole symbol but then  you can keep going and click and you'll  make a triple click just clicking very  fast in succession actually a small bug  here  and what it does then is it it extends  the selection in in a way where it's  aware of the structure so you know  instead of selecting the whole paragraph  at once which is what usual text editors  would do it just looks at the outer  expression and keeps extending it that  way so if a triple click I get that  whole thing and then if I keep going it  will you know to be fast enough it will  select something like this so especially  if you have large nested nested  expressions this is really useful to  figure out where they start and end  basically that being said there is still  some work to do so one thing that  doesn't work quite well yet is if you  have explicit new lines in your  expressions that something we still need  to fix and also it's not necessarily  aware of all the operator presidencies  so in in desktop Mathematica if you let  see something like this it would be  aware that the times operator it binds  you know stronger than the plus and and  the multi click selection would takes it  into account here it doesn't work that  perfectly yet so if a triple click this  it selects only this part when it should  really select the first part as well so  that's a minor thing we still need to  improve as well but at least in terms of  function calls and arguments it's it's  aware of that structure another sort of  user assist features feature is spell  checking also coming in 144 so if it's  not enabled by default for you you can  go to your user preferences and and  activate spell checking and then what  you should see is in this case where  it's not really English text anyway it  makes a lot of red underlines indicating  misspelled words you get suggestions how  to correct them or you could also ignore  individual instance  of misspellings again something that we  still need to do is have user-defined  dictionaries so that you can add words  to a dictionary and then that's you know  never flag this as misspelled it's  something we still need to implement and  of course it would be nice to share  dictionaries between your cloud account  and desktop Mathematica as well okay  that's some of the new features coming  soon and and these features are mostly  related to editing notebooks in the  cloud and so so in that sense cloud  notebooks are the primary interface to  what's going on in in your cloud account  and you can use them to deploy other  things like API functions and so on if  you don't have tests of Mathematica  especially but but cloud notebooks are  also the underlying technology for  notebooks deployed to the cloud that you  think can can share with other users  even if you don't edit them there and so  the question is how do you get your  content into the cloud the most  straightforward way is to just use cloud  deployed so if I have a some content  like this manipulate here I can just  call cloud deploy and it will put that  into the cloud in its one argument form  it will just produce an unnamed object  but most of the time if you want to  share things with other users you  probably want a nicer URL and then the  second argument is useful where you can  specify the path where it should go to  in your cloud account and also if you  want to share things important' to set  permissions there's a very powerful and  complex structure of possible  permissions in this case we're basically  saying that everyone even unauthentic  unauthenticated users should be able to  both read the notebook and also interact  with it and once we do that we get back  a cloud object and you can open this  link  and it will show me this this  manipulative course where I can now  interact with it and it just works as  expected I wanted to point out one thing  that claw deployed tries to be somewhat  smart about what object it produces and  you can tell if you know things like API  function and form function of course it  does something different depending on  what expression you give it and it also  does it to some extent with sort of  notebook content in this case it  produced an interactive notebook but if  you have a very static piece of content  like a grid and you deploy this one what  it produces still a cloud object of  course but it looks quite different  little bigger  too weak so you can see that it looks  very different from what we had here in  the notebook and the reason is that it  didn't actually produce a notebook  there's a little you know caveat here if  you look at the type of this static  object we can see that it's actually not  a notebook but it's this thing called  cloud CDF dot HTML which really means  that it produced a static piece of HTML  instead of just a notebook and sometimes  that's probably useful because you know  if you have some simple content you  might not want all the overhead that  comes with with interactive notebooks  necessarily but if you if you absolutely  want a notebook there is there still a  way to do that and by the way that that  HTML that it produced that's equivalent  to what you get by the HTML fragment  export format so you know what it  produced is basically this simple HTML  table and that we put that into a cloud  object and again if you  if you want an actual notebook there is  a way to do that you just need to be a  bit more explicit so instead of using  cloud deploy you can use cloud export  where you can specify the format that  should be produced and in this case we  say we want a notebook and then again we  just say where the cloud object should  be generated if I do this we get a cloud  object and this this is now a notebook  that looks much more similar to what we  had you know in our notebook in the  first place right so that's a little  thing to keep in mind when working with  cloud deploy also something that maybe  not explained very well in our  documentation yet but it's certainly  good to be aware of is the concept of  views in the cloud  we make a pretty fundamental distinction  between what we call the editor or  product view and the deployed view so  the editor view as the name suggests is  the the view where you edit your content  in the cloud usually so if you go to a  product like the developer platform or  mathematic online or so from one what  you see is is this edit of view where  usually you have a session kernel that's  tied to your browser session which means  that it's actually shared between  multiple notebooks if you have no  several cloud notebooks at the same time  if you have them open at the same time  in the same browser in the developer  platform for instance they actually  share a single kernel and if you you  know create a symbol in one notebook  that symbol will be available in the  other node as well pretty much like it  works on on desktop Mathematica by  default so that's your session kernel it  does expire after a certain time of  inactivity and then you just have to  refresh your session since it's tied to  products it's it does require a product  subscription usually for whatever  product you're using which also means  that it's requiring authentic  so anytime you want to go to product  view you have to to login doesn't  necessarily mean that you have to pay  for this because there's free  subscriptions to to most of the products  but at least you have to authenticate  and that's in contrast to the deployed  view which is what you get when you go  to www.irs.gov that notebook if you  visit that again was it just so-so here  can see the URL I can actually change  this on the fly so I can go to the  developer platform product view but just  changing this subdomain here and it's  still the same notebook same cloud  object but now I'm in a different view  where I could also edit this notebook  for instance so that's that's that  sometimes useful and yeah so it's just a  different view on the same notebook and  in a deployed view again it's it's we  are meant for public consumption so what  you get there is what we call a public  kernel it's the same thing that also API  functions use there's no real concept of  sessions or at least only short-lived  sessions and it also means that whatever  evaluations happen there they they  consume cloud credits of the owner of  the notebook since it's not using your  session call that you have anyway but  you know might have to spin up a lot of  kernels well we have to charge you some  some cloud credits for that again that's  made for public consumption so it does  not necessarily require authentication  it still depends on the permissions of  course so if if you have a private cloud  object it will of course not be visible  in the deployed view either  but what it means is that if you have a  public object like this one here it's to  unauthenticated users it's only visible  in the deployed view and they can't use  the product view of course so this is  the thing you want to use if you want to  share notebooks broadly or maybe embed  them on a website or something like that  just one little thing to point out in  terms of sessions  that's another improvement that we've  made recently which will be released a  little later this year we've made the  whole concept of notebook sessions a bit  more robust so what you won't see  anymore is this this error message that  says session timed out due to inactivity  and then you have to refresh the whole  page which was always a bit annoying so  instead what happens is that even if you  know the session terminates on the  server after some time we can just  resume it basically and you can keep  working on the notebook without  reloading the whole page that being said  the kernel session itself that still  expires after a while so and you will  still get a little notification about  that but it will it's much less  disruptive so if you know if the kernel  session expires you make another  evaluation it will just spin up a new  kernel on the fly you know finally some  some even longer term look out what  we're starting to work on now is a more  powerful box editor for math type  setting and also grids and things like  that on one hand and on the other hand  we're starting to work on live  collaboration on notebooks so that if  multiple users have write permission on  a notebook they can all edit it at the  same time there's still a lot of  questions to be answered about that but  but we're making some progress and  especially if you're interested in that  have some use cases for for either of  these  please let me know and we're out of time  now so thank you very much  [Applause]

