tform product view but just  changing this subdomain here and it's  still the same notebook same cloud  object but now I'm in a different view  where I could also edit this notebook  for instance so that's that's that  sometimes useful and yeah so it's just a  different view on the same notebook and  in a deployed view again it's it's we  are meant for public consumption so what  you get there is what we call a public  kernel it's the same thing that also API  functions use there's no real concept of  sessions or at least only short-lived  sessions and it also means that whatever  evaluations happen there they they  consume cloud credits of the owner of  the notebook since it's not using your  session call that you have anyway but  you know might have to spin up a lot of  kernels well we have to charge you some  some cloud credits for that again that's  made for public consumption so it does  not necessarily require authentication  it still depends on the permissions of  course so if if you have a private cloud  object it will of course not be visible  in the deployed view either  but what it means is that if you have a  public object like this one here it's to  unauthenticated users it's only visible  in the deployed view and they can't use  the product view of course so this is  the thing you want to use if you want to  share notebooks broadly or maybe embed  them on a website or something like that  just one little thing to point out in  terms of sessions  that's another improvement that we've  made recently which will be released a  little later this year we've made the  whole concept of notebook sessions a bit  more robust so what you won't see  anymore is this this error message that  says session timed out due to inactivity  and then you have to refresh the whole  page which was always a bit annoying so  instead what happens is that even if you  know the session terminates on the  server after some time we can just  resume it basically and you can keep  working on the notebook without  reloading the whole page that being said  the kernel session itself that still  expires after a while so and you will  still get a little notification about  that but it will it's much less  disruptive so if you know if the kernel  session expires you make another  evaluation it will just spin up a new  kernel on the fly you know finally some  some even longer term look out what  we're starting to work on now is a more  powerful box editor for math type  setting and also grids and things like  that on one hand and on the other hand  we're starting to work on live  collaboration on notebooks so that if  multiple users have write permission on  a notebook they can all edit it at the  same time there's still a lot of  questions to be answered about that but  but we're making some progress and  especially if you're interested in that  have some use cases for for either of  these  please let me know and we're out of time  now so thank you very much  [Applause]
 

